WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
<v Mike>North and South, your combat prowess and brilliant strategy could rewrite history.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:30.220
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.380
<v Hosts>I'm Mike.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.360
<v Hosts>And I'm Sean.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.840
<v Mike>You could rewrite history.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.320
<v Sean>Uh huh. Yeah.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.840
<v Mike>Is that really like, is that the selling point of the game?

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.740
<v Mike>Did you have the chance to right some wrongs?

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:53.480
<v Sean>No. I don't, I think it's just for people that are very interested in that time period, Mike.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.800
<v Mike>Agreed, agreed.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:02.340
<v Mike>North and South is a mashup of a turn-based strategy game and real-time action.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.880
<v Mike>What I'll be calling party games.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.640
<v Mike>Uh, because I want to make this sound like Mario Party as much as possible throughout the episode.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.200
<v Mike>But it's all wrapped under a civil war campaign.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:21.860
<v Mike>That's right. The North and South that are in the title of the game are referring to the North and the South, uh, fighting in the Civil War between the years.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:22.960
<v Sean>The American Civil War.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.860
<v Mike>Right. The American Civil War, 1861 to 1864.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:36.340
<v Mike>Uh, you know, it, it basically approached, the game is approached from a risk light, which is funny because we were talking about risk last week for no good reason.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:45.700
<v Mike>But you have this map where, uh, you know, each state is a, uh, is a territory up for grabs by both the North and the South.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.480
<v Mike>And, uh, you want to, just like in risk, you want to conquer all the territories.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.020
<v Mike>You want to do that here.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.340
<v Mike>But in order to do so, it isn't just, uh, my number's bigger than your number, roll some dice thing.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:06.300
<v Mike>It's actually, uh, each, each time you try to take something over, turns into a little party game where the armies collide.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.400
<v Mike>Or you try to, uh, steal the railroad.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.640
<v Mike>Or you try to infiltrate a, uh, uh, uh, what, what is that?

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.520
<v Mike>Is that just like a town?

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.600
<v Sean>Is that a, is that?

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.780
<v Sean>It's a fort. Okay.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.440
<v Mike>Yeah. Try to infiltrate the fort.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.920
<v Mike>Uh, all these little things to just spice up the gameplay a little bit.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.740
<v Mike>So it isn't just risk or it isn't just strategy.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.980
<v Mike>They're adding, they're adding some video game components to this.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:36.200
<v Mike>Otherwise, what would be if we're looking at some of our grander strategy games that we've played by Koei.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.680
<v Mike>This isn't that.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:47.780
<v Mike>So first question is the main appeal, the strategy layer, or is it really the fact that the strategy is just a way to trigger these mini games?

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.560
<v Sean>Um, that's a good question.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:03:01.920
<v Sean>Um, I, I would say that, yes, I think my, I think the main appeal is the strategy because I don't think that there's any real depth in the mini games.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:12.860
<v Sean>Um, and if I can, if I may, I think that this is finally the kind of strategy game that I was looking for on the system.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:31.900
<v Sean>Um, I think I got a little bit ahead of myself when I said at one point that I was looking forward to something such as, um, uh, Nobunaga's ambition or romance, the three kingdoms or Genghis Khan or whatever the hell else we played recently.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.800
<v Sean>Um, this is what, this is more what I'm looking for.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:43.220
<v Sean>Uh, something that has strategy, um, but isn't just a giant spreadsheet.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.100
<v Mike>Yes.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.440
<v Mike>Agreed. Uh, the one we played most recently was Bandit Kings of Ancient China.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:55.000
<v Mike>I remind you, sounds like a, uh, a song wings would have wrote, uh, in the late seventies.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.220
<v Mike>Uh, yes, I agree with you.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:04.360
<v Mike>I think that, uh, especially on an eight bit system, this makes more sense, but I don't want you to forget.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.260
<v Mike>And I'm pretty sure back then you were, you were playing all the games too.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.800
<v Mike>So I think you were on the episode defender of the crown.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.480
<v Mike>That was kind of a game like this.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.060
<v Mike>That was like strategy adjacent.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.420
<v Mike>There weren't like the party.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:17.500
<v Mike>Uh, I I'm sorry.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.840
<v Mike>I'm not, I can't call them party games any longer.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.540
<v Mike>I really wanted to force it, but they're really just like light mini games.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.460
<v Mike>Um, that do have, that do have fail states.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.860
<v Mike>Like you, it's not just like there.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.540
<v Sean>And there is depth to the one-on-one combat.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.760
<v Sean>Not so much the raid the train, but yeah, there's depth there.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.500
<v Mike>But it's all everything.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:45.080
<v Mike>Both the, the fact that there is a civil war, the fact that, um, you conquer these states,

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.900
<v Mike>the fact that you fight off in a war, it's all kind of done under this, uh, cartoonish,

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:58.920
